Jahshaka patch 0070 — the VCT cone's start bias is ONE CELL, whichever way the
surface faces (MEDIA ONLY)

WHAT IS WRONG UPSTREAM
----------------------
Every voxel-cone-tracing cone — the six diffuse ones and the specular one, in the
pixel piece, and the diffuse set again in the bounce-injection compute twin —
starts a short distance off the surface so that it does not immediately sample
the voxel the surface itself occupies. Upstream expresses that distance as

    posLS += (dirLS / maxVal) * vctInvResolution * lerp( 15.0f, 1.0f, maxVal )

where `dirLS` is the geometric normal in probe space and `maxVal` is its largest
component. For a normal pointing down an axis maxVal = 1 and the step is ONE
voxel. For a body-diagonal normal maxVal = 1/sqrt(3) = 0.577, so lerp(15,1,0.577)
= 6.92 and the division by maxVal makes it 6.92/0.577 = TWELVE voxels. The
compute twin has the same shape without the lerp: one voxel to 1.73.

The stated reason is staircase aliasing on axis-aligned steps, and on a BOX it is
invisible: every face has maxVal = 1 and every cone starts one voxel out.

On a CURVED surface it is a seam by construction. A sphere's normal sweeps every
direction, so the start point travels from one voxel to twelve and back across the
six arcs where two components of the normal are equal in magnitude — 8 cm against
94 cm on a 7.8 cm cascade. A downward-diagonal normal on the lower half of a
sphere starts its specular cone most of a metre INSIDE the floor and reads the
floor's contact shadow; the upward-diagonal normal beside it starts above the
sphere's own lit voxels and reads those. The result is a bright warped streak
beside a dark smear, curved along the arc.

That is the artefact the owner's smoke of push #41 reported on a plain sphere in
an empty scene (spikes/smoke-41/screenshot7.png) — at the document's default
roughness both the screen-space and the ray-traced reflection arms are gated off,
so this cone IS the whole reflection of that surface.

THE FIX
-------
A start bias is a geometric statement — "leave the surface's own voxel before
sampling" — and a geometric statement cannot depend on how the surface happens to
sit against the lattice. So the step becomes ONE CELL along the normal, measured
along the normal, everywhere:

    biasDirLS = normalise( dirLS )
    posLS    += biasDirLS * dot( abs( biasDirLS ), vctInvResolution.xyz )

An axis-aligned surface is unchanged (it always got exactly one cell). Everything
else stops sweeping. The same expression replaces the same term at the two cascade
HOPS in the pixel piece (the diffuse walk and the specular walk each re-enter the
next cascade one bias along the normal, in that cascade's units), and in the
bounce-injection compute shader.

FILES
-----
  Samples/Media/Hlms/Pbs/Any/Vct_piece_ps.any        (3 sites)
  Samples/Media/VCT/LightVctBounceInject_piece_cs.any (1 site)

MEDIA ONLY. Studio stages media from the SOURCE tree (cmake/IncludeOgre.cmake), so
no C++ is recompiled — but every tree still re-runs the patch loop, and a tree
whose staged media predates this patch renders the old bias.

OVERLAP. Vct_piece_ps.any is also touched by patches 0021, 0033, 0045, 0048 and
0066; build-ogre.sh's per-patch reverse check cannot see an overlap, so a tree
that already carries those will report this one as "upstream changed the patched
file". Reset the submodule and apply the stack in order — the standing recipe.

